.TH ECHO 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
echo \- write arguments to standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B echo
.RB [ \-neE ]
.RI [ string " ...]"
.br
.B echo
.BR \-\-help | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B echo
writes its
.I string
operands to standard output, separated by single spaces and followed
by a newline.
.PP
Leading arguments that consist of
.B \-
followed only by the letters
.BR n ,
.BR e ,
and
.B E
are taken as options; they may be combined, as in
.BR \-ne .
Option processing stops at the first other argument, which is printed
together with everything after it.
There is no
.B \-\-
end-of-options marker;
.B \-\-
is printed like any other string.
.SS Escape sequences
With
.BR \-e ,
the following backslash sequences are interpreted:
.TP
.B \e\e
backslash
.TP
.B \ea
alert (BEL)
.TP
.B \eb
backspace
.TP
.B \ec
produce no further output: the rest of the argument, any further
arguments, and the trailing newline are suppressed
.TP
.BR \ee ", " \eE
escape (ESC, 0x1B)
.TP
.B \ef
form feed
.TP
.B \en
newline
.TP
.B \er
carriage return
.TP
.B \et
horizontal tab
.TP
.B \ev
vertical tab
.TP
.BI \e0 nnn
the byte with octal value
.I nnn
(zero to three octal digits)
.TP
.BI \ex hh
the byte with hexadecimal value
.I hh
(one or two hex digits)
.TP
.BI \eu hhhh
the Unicode character U+\fIhhhh\fP, written as UTF-8 (exactly four hex
digits)
.TP
.BI \eU hhhhhhhh
the Unicode character U+\fIhhhhhhhh\fP, written as UTF-8 (exactly
eight hex digits)
.PP
Any other backslash sequence, a
.B \ex
with no hex digit, or a
.B \eu
or
.B \eU
with too few digits or naming a surrogate or a value above U+10FFFF,
is written unchanged.
A backslash at the end of an argument is written as is.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Do not write the trailing newline.
.TP
.B \-e
Interpret backslash escape sequences.
.TP
.B \-E
Do not interpret backslash escape sequences (the default).
When both
.B \-e
and
.B \-E
are given, the last one wins.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A write to standard output failed.
A write failing with
.B EPIPE
exits with status 1 without printing a diagnostic.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, only
.B \-n
is recognised as an option (and may be repeated);
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-E ,
.BR \-\-help ,
and
.B \-\-version
are printed as ordinary strings, and escape sequences are not
interpreted.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print a line without a trailing newline:
.PP
.EX
$ echo \-n "prompt> "
.EE
.PP
Print a tab-separated line:
.PP
.EX
$ echo \-e "a\etb\etc"
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR printf (3)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024 leaves
.B \-n
and backslashes implementation-defined.
The
.B \-e
and
.B \-E
options and the escape set follow GNU coreutils.
